    #    would be difficult to serialize.
    top_level_unique_values_dict = {'8c145dcc-95f7-489e-ba66-9fa0982b731b': pickle.loads(b"\x80\x04\x95z\x15\x00\x00\x00\x00\x00\x00Xs\x15\x00\x00# READ ME FIRST: Module 7, 2 Exercise 1: Read the topology\n\n**Griptape Enterprise:** Exercises 1 and 2 are about the Admin Server and the Admin Dashboard, which come with Griptape Enterprise. Exercise 1 only reads a file and Exercise 2 shows its screens, so you can follow both without it. Exercise 3 works in every install.\n\n**One config file, one seat, one frame.** The Admin Server is a gatekeeper on your own network: every call to Griptape Cloud passes through it. First you read what it lets out of your network. Then you issue a licence key with a policy (a set of rules) on it, and watch the rules hold on a second machine. That machine takes one seat: one person or machine using a licence at a time. Last, you follow a finished frame back to the graph that made it, meaning the nodes, their connections and their settings.\n\n## Where this fits\n\n**Before this:** **Module 7, 1 Sources**.\n\n**Next:** **Module 7, 3 Exercise 2: Issue a seat**.\n\n## Not just the exercises\n- **The file is the answer.** What leaves the building is decided by a few lines in the Admin Server's settings file, config.yaml, not by what a vendor promises.\n- **A key is a policy.** A licence key is added to access groups (one for a team or a show, say), and each group carries permission templates: the rules for what a group of people may use. When one rule allows something and another sets it to **Deny**, the deny always wins.\n- **A PNG carries its graph.** A PNG holds the whole graph that made it, tucked inside the file. Every file saved through the Project, PNG or not, also gets a small JSON record in the **.griptape-nodes-metadata** folder next to the workflow file. Nothing warns you either way.\n- **Your answers live in the Project.** Each worksheet is a Text Input you save into the outputs folder, so the security review gets a file, not a memory.\n\n## The canvas\n- **Start here** (this box): how to work through the module, plus videos, help pages and the model note.\n- **Exercise 1** (right): numbered step boxes say what to do and what to look for.\n\n## Working through it\n**The results of a full run are already in:** every picture and video shows as soon as you open the template, with nothing to run. The steps say which node to press play on: do that only to make your own version, since running nodes yourself uses credits. Your four records come with their template text; after you fill one in, press play on its save node. Exercise 2 happens in the Admin Dashboard in your browser, not on the canvas.\n\n1. **Exercise 1, on paper.** Read *Your Default Config*. Change *Your Allow Config* to the **allow** mode, where only the paths you name get through. Answer the questions in *Your Topology Answers*, then press play on the two save nodes.\n\n## Keys\n- **Tab** (or **Shift+A**) with the cursor over the canvas: add a node there. Type its app name, the first part of every node title here, such as **Save Text**.\n- **Ctrl+Enter**: run the selected node, and whatever it needs that has not run yet. **Ctrl+Shift+Enter**: run the whole workflow.\n- **Ctrl+S**: save. **F**: frame the selected node (with nothing selected, the whole workflow). **Space** (hold): pan the canvas. **Delete** or **Backspace**: remove the selected node or connection.\n- **Help, Keyboard Shortcuts** (**Shift+?**) lists the main keys. On a Mac, use **Cmd** where it says **Ctrl**.\n\n## Prerequisites\n- **Exercise 1:** nothing to install. The Admin Server documentation is in the *Read* note.\n- **Exercise 2:** a Griptape Cloud account that **owns an organization** (the Admin Dashboard never appears for a seat activated with a licence key), and a **second machine** with Griptape Nodes installed and signed out, so it shows the login screen.\n- **Exercise 3:** a Griptape account with credits for one image.\n- **Model choices and costs:** see the *Models* note on the right.\n\n## Where your files go\n- **Sources** go into the **sources/module\\_7** folder inside **{outputs}**. The exercises read the newest file there.\n- Every file is saved into this Project's **outputs** folder: **{outputs}/text/module\\_7/exercise\\_1/**, **exercise\\_2/** and **exercise\\_3/** for the worksheets, **{outputs}/images/module\\_7/exercise\\_3/** for the frames. The Project adds **\\_v001**, **\\_v002** ... so nothing is overwritten.\n- Exercise 3's load nodes read the files of the last full run. After a run of your own, change their *path* to the newest number.\n\n## Good to know\n- **There is no offline mode.** A licence needs a way out to Griptape Cloud. If that way is down, nobody can start a new session.\n- **Scaling today:** a workflow with a Start Flow and an End Flow saves as a Python file you can run from the command line, with one setting per input. **Publish Workflow**, then **Publish To Folder**, writes a folder that holds everything it needs, with its own run script. A licence key can be **Headless**, for machines that run with nobody at them. Inside one engine, nodes run one at a time by default: set the setting **workflow\\_execution\\_mode** to **parallel** (**Settings**, **Settings**, then search for it) and up to **max\\_nodes\\_in\\_parallel** nodes, 5 by default, run at once. Out of the box, nothing in the app schedules or queues work, or sends it to a render farm: your pipeline or an add-on library such as Deadline Cloud has to do that. Module 5 walks through the headless run (a run without the window).\n- Run one node with its own play button: it runs that node and whatever it needs that has not run yet.\x94."), 'cbd44941-195a-40e3-b102-78134841186f': pickle.loads(b'\x80\x04\x95r\x00\x00\x00\x00\x00\x00\x00\x8cn# Watch\n- [What is Griptape Enterprise?](https://youtu.be/BJIg8opcX7c?t=124): the admin walkthrough, from 2:04\x94.'), '156f6e44-8cea-48fc-82d2-900ff2419c33': pickle.loads(b'\x80\x04\x95\xfb\x02\x00\x00\x00\x00\x00\x00X\xf4\x02\x00\x00# Read\n- [Admin Server](https://docs.griptapenodes.com/en/stable/enterprise/admin_server/): config.yaml, forwarding modes, required routes\n- [Using the Admin Server](https://docs.griptapenodes.com/en/stable/enterprise/using_the_admin_server/): activating against it\n- [Admin Dashboard](https://docs.griptapenodes.com/en/stable/enterprise/admin_dashboard/): keys, groups, templates, sessions\n- [Cedar policies](https://docs.griptapenodes.com/en/stable/enterprise/cedar_policies/): templates written by hand\n- [Architecture](https://docs.griptapenodes.com/en/stable/architecture/): what talks to Griptape Cloud, and the five routes\n- [Publishing](https://docs.griptapenodes.com/en/stable/guides/publishing/): what a published bundle carries, secrets included\x94.'), '4ee9aa5e-a861-4d12-8f1a-ce39922f0116': pickle.loads(b'\x80\x04\x95\xcc\x00\x00\x00\x00\x00\x00\x00\x8c\xc8# Open\nTemplates in the app:\n- **Prompt an Image**: the smallest working workflow, where Exercise 3 starts\n\n## Next\nModule 7 is the last module: back to the overview of all seven on the training site.\x94.'), 'f6890238-6435-4601-8a56-382751ebcba0': pickle.loads(b"\x80\x04\x95+\x03\x00\x00\x00\x00\x00\x00X$\x03\x00\x00# Models: why these\n\nOnly Exercise 3 runs a model. It uses the same small image model as Module 1, picked so the exercise runs **the day you install Griptape Nodes**. The point is the files it leaves behind, not the picture.\n\n| Job | Model | Why this one | Cost, 1 Oct 2026 |\n|---|---|---|---|\n| The frame (Exercise 3) | GPT Image 1 Mini | Small, fast and cheap | about $0.01 per image (estimate from OpenAI's token price) |\n\n**The whole workflow, run once (estimate, 1 Oct 2026):** about $0.01.\n\n**About the costs:** estimates in dollars. Griptape shows credit in dollars too: your balance at the top of the app, and each model's own price under **Manage Subscription**, **Pricing** (platform fees included). Prices change, so check your credit balance before and after a run to see what it really cost.\x94."), 'ccf11c2a-a1db-4669-b25d-ed4ac4e31829': pickle.loads(b'\x80\x04\x95.\x02\x00\x00\x00\x00\x00\x00X\'\x02\x00\x00server:\n  host: "0.0.0.0"\n  port: 8080\n  read_header_timeout: "10s"\n  write_stall_timeout: "60s"\n  idle_timeout: "120s"\n  shutdown_timeout: "10s"\n\nupstream:\n  base_url: "https://cloud.griptape.ai"\n  timeout: "120s"\n  # Name of the environment variable that holds the Griptape Cloud API key.\n  # The key value is never stored here, only the variable name.\n  api_key_env: "GT_CLOUD_API_KEY"\n\nlogging:\n  level: "info"   # debug | info | warn | error\n  format: "json"  # json | text\n\nforwarding:\n  mode: "allow_all"  # allow_all | allow | deny\n  rules: []\x94.'), 'eea1c313-f52e-4fd5-892a-bb8ca2006959': pickle.loads(b'\x80\x04\x95a\x00\x00\x00\x00\x00\x00\x00\x8c1griptape_nodes.common.project_templates.situation\x94\x8c\x10BuiltInSituation\x94\x93\x94\x8c\x10save_node_output\x94\x85\x94R\x94.'), '2f72fa0a-a563-459e-95aa-22991a4a3ff0': pickle.loads(b'\x80\x04\x955\x00\x00\x00\x00\x00\x00\x00\x8c1module_7/exercise_1/admin_server_config_allow.txt\x94.'), '118e5cd7-1c0c-4575-af6f-64cf933b961d': pickle.loads(b'\x80\x04\x95-\x03\x00\x00\x00\x00\x00\x00X&\x03\x00\x00Your answers. Write under each question, then press play on Save Your Topology Answers.\n\n1. The one host the Admin Server talks to:\n\n2. The five required routes. For each: what it does, and what breaks without it. (Architecture page, On-premises configuration, in the Read note.)\n   /api/sessions/* :\n   /api/session-renew :\n   /api/session-release :\n   /api/users :\n   /api/organizations :\n\n3. After your rewrite to allow: can a workflow still reach a model through Griptape Cloud? Where exactly does the refusal come from, and what does the application get back?\n\n4. Which path carries prompts to the Griptape Cloud model proxy, and did you leave it open?\n\n5. Three kinds of traffic the forwarding list cannot touch:\n\n6. What the file on disk does not show you (hint: how else can every setting be set?):\x94.'), '48e77983-a7ab-4aa0-a980-2a5cfe5515d6': pickle.loads(b'\x80\x04\x95,\x00\x00\x00\x00\x00\x00\x00\x8c(module_7/exercise_1/topology_answers.txt\x94.'), '9dfa6b3f-af45-46a5-b21f-062175c6afdb': pickle.loads(b'\x80\x04\x95g\x00\x00\x00\x00\x00\x00\x00\x8cc**Read this first.** What the workflow does, how to run it, what you need, and where to learn more.\x94.'), '539779e9-ec49-4b42-955d-344792946429': pickle.loads(b'\x80\x04\x95S\x02\x00\x00\x00\x00\x00\x00XL\x02\x00\x00**Griptape Enterprise only:** the Admin Server comes with Griptape Enterprise ([request it from Foundry](https://www.foundry.com/products/griptape/request-demo)). This exercise only reads its settings file, so anyone can do it.\n\nA paper exercise about one file: **config.yaml**, the settings file the Admin Server reads when it starts. Work out what it lets through and what it stops. Then make the forwarding mode (the rule for which calls it passes on to Griptape Cloud) stricter, and answer again. Last, name the traffic that never goes through it at all.\n\n**Focus:** reading the file.\x94.'), 'b855ded5-f2cf-43ae-9e58-eb8a2503a086': pickle.loads(b'\x80\x04\x95\xd9\x05\x00\x00\x00\x00\x00\x00X\xd2\x05\x00\x00**Why:** in **allow** mode the Admin Server passes on only the paths you list, and everything else stops there. Five licensing routes (paths on Griptape Cloud that the licence uses) are the minimum the product cannot run without.\n\n**How:** *Your Allow Config* starts as a copy of the default. Edit it in place, then press play on *Save Your Allow Config*.\n\n**Look for:** compare your file with the **allow** example in the Admin Server docs (Read note, Configuration, forwarding), but end every rule with a slash even where the docs leave it off: in allow mode a rule without one matches only that exact path. If your list leaves out a required route, the server will not start, and it tells you which route is missing. A timeout with no unit (**30** instead of **30s**) also stops it from starting. A zero in quotes (**"0s"**) switches off the read, write-stall and idle timeouts; the shutdown and upstream timeouts must stay above zero.\n\n**Saved to:** **{outputs}/text/module\\_7/exercise\\_1/admin\\_server\\_config\\_allow\\_v001.txt**. Rename the copy you hand to IT **config.yaml**.\n\n**Your turn:** set **mode** to **allow**. Under **rules**, delete the empty brackets and list exactly these five, one per line, each as a dash and the path in quotes, each ending in a slash: **/api/sessions/**, **/api/session-renew/**, **/api/session-release/**, **/api/users/**, **/api/organizations/**. Leave out **/api/proxy/**, the model proxy, which passes prompts on to models through Griptape Cloud.\x94.'), '0bfee92e-8bbc-4140-a408-b54641412b07': pickle.loads(b'\x80\x04\x95\xae\x03\x00\x00\x00\x00\x00\x00X\xa7\x03\x00\x00**Why:** these are the questions a security review asks. Answer them from the file and the docs, not from memory.\n\n**How:** write under each question in *Your Topology Answers*, then press play on *Save Your Topology Answers*. The Architecture page (Read note) has a table of the five routes.\n\n**Look for:** a call to a path you have not permitted gets its answer from the Admin Server itself: **403** and **path not permitted**. It never reaches Griptape Cloud. The list only covers Griptape Cloud. These never go through the Admin Server at all: model calls to a provider with your own API key, local models (Ollama, LM Studio), library installs from GitHub, and model downloads from Hugging Face. Every setting can also be set by an environment variable (a value handed to the program when it starts, outside the file), and that wins over the file.\n\n**Saved to:** **{outputs}/text/module\\_7/exercise\\_1/topology\\_answers\\_v001.txt**\x94.'), 'a1bbb452-aa77-4a3a-8bc7-768b7032cfaf': pickle.loads(b'\x80\x04\x95\x15\x02\x00\x00\x00\x00\x00\x00X\x0e\x02\x00\x00**Why:** this file decides what leaves the network. A diagram does not.\n\n**How:** the file has four blocks. **server** is the address and port where the Admin Server waits for calls from your machines. **upstream** is the one host it talks to. **logging** is how it writes down each request. **forwarding** is a mode and a list of paths: which calls it passes on.\n\n**Look for:** the upstream **base\\_url**, and the mode **allow\\_all**: every path is passed on.\n\n**Focus:** the forwarding block. The other three are just setup.\x94.')}
